<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Birchtown</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Birchtown is a community near Shelburne in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, founded in 1783 by about 1,500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-loyalists">Black Loyalists</a> who had escaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> to support the British in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a>. It was then the largest free Black settlement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. Hardship and broken promises of land led many of its people to leave for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a> in 1792.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Birchtown was named after Brigadier General Samuel Birch, a British official who helped lead the evacuation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-loyalists">Black Loyalists</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. He recorded the settlers' names in the Book of Negroes and signed the passports, known as General Birch Certificates, that established their freedom. Most arrived through the nearby town of Port Roseway, soon renamed Shelburne. The community was first settled by Stephen Blucke, called the true founder of the Afro-Nova Scotian community, and its core was five companies of the Black Pioneers.</p><p>More than two thirds of the Black population who came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> were from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-south">American South</a>. Newspapers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> described Birchtown as the largest settlement of free <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> in the world. Its population grew in July 1784, when white residents attacked free Black people in Shelburne in the Shelburne Riots, and many, among them the clergyman David George, fled to Birchtown for safety.</p><p>Poor land, inadequate supplies, a harsh climate, discrimination and broken promises led residents under Thomas Peters to petition the British government, and many joined the migration of 1792 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a>; their descendants form part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone-creole-people">Sierra Leone Creole people</a>. Those who stayed became ancestors of the Black Nova Scotian population of Shelburne County, in a small community of a few hundred living by farming, fishing and forestry.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Birchtown was declared a National Historic Site in 1997, when the Black Loyalist Heritage Society opened a seasonal museum that included the historic school and church. An arson attack in 2006 destroyed much of its offices and archives. In June 2015 the Black Loyalist Heritage Centre opened, telling the story of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-loyalists">Black Loyalists</a> in America, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Birchtown, Nova Scotia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After 1783 tens of thousands of Loyalists arrived, including Black Loyalists at <b>Birchtown</b>, and New Brunswick was split off in 1784.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-nova-scotia">Colony of Nova Scotia</a></li></ul></section>
